      "content": "You have the right formula! To keep the substitution clear and avoid mixing up the numbers, you can use this simple memory rule: **\"X's together, Y's together, subtract, square, and add under the roof.\"**\n\nLet's apply this to $P(7; 4)$ and $Q(6; 6)$. If we let $P$ be $(x_1; y_1)$ and $Q$ be $(x_2; y_2)$, what do you get when you substitute the $x$-values and $y$-values into the formula?"
